experiment: echopred
  SHORT RUN — 300000 ticks against a minimum of 672000. derived: §66. A GATE on a genome change, not the change. §65 named a
  combiner as the next build; this prices the one the architecture can
  cheaply support. DNA v15's rectified per-channel subtraction already
  exists in hear(); what was refuted is its prediction SOURCE (central
  carries no linear information about the sound at any tract density), and
  the shipped previous-frame predictor sees only silence across the gap.
  So the prediction is sourced from an efference copy of the creature's own
  posture, which §63 measured holding the first word at 0.7727. Rendered
  host-side through a second VowelSource and a second Cochlea: NO GENOME
  CHANGE. A refusal is the useful outcome and kills the combiner cheaply.
  Everything below is printed for debugging and is NOT a
  measurement; this run cannot pass whatever it reads.


  §66 — CAN AN EFFERENCE COPY CANCEL A MATCHING WORD?

  the question    §65 named a COMBINER as the next build. This prices the
                  one the architecture can cheaply support, BEFORE the
                  genome is touched.
  the mechanism   DNA v15's rectified per-channel subtraction in hear(),
                  but sourced from the ARTICULATORS instead of central --
                  central was refuted as a prediction source, and the
                  shipped previous-frame predictor sees only silence
                  across the gap.
  how             a SECOND VowelSource renders the creature's own posture
                  and a SECOND Cochlea turns it into mel. No genome change.
  the prediction  rendered at a FIXED amplitude (0.50): quietecho measured
                  the posture identical at 94% and 0% phonation, so it is
                  a shape that exists whether or not it is being voiced.
  the bar         0.650, three-way.
  creatures       12

